  "title": "CHAPTER 2: GEOGRAPHY OF KERALA",
  "order": 2,
  "blocks": [
    {
      "id": "v2aayv9",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "2.1 Physical Features"
    },
    {
      "type": "keyFacts",
      "title": "Key Takeaways",
      "items": [
        "Kerala's physical terrain is divided into three distinct natural zones: Lowlands, Midlands, and Highlands.",
        "The drainage network consists of 44 major rivers, with 41 flowing west into the Arabian Sea and 3 flowing east.",
        "Lakes and backwaters (Kayals) form a unique interconnected network along the coastal lowland region.",
        "The Western Ghats border the eastern side, containing high mountain peaks, dense forests, and wildlife sanctuaries.",
        "The state is administratively structured into 14 districts, each possessing distinct geographical and climatic variations."
      ],
      "id": "y6k0c3g"
    },
